{
  "dom_id": "step-q66873",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Expressed in terms of common logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${10}^{x-2}={3}^{x}?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x-2} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x-2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx-2=\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-2=x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & =2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
